#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151866 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41a9d47a-2dd6-5177-afd1-e4adcd887f55" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "150649f4-8b1e-563a-a774-1857d8781212" :question/math-academy-id "e-6247" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,2]\\times [1,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x+2y$. Further, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$." :question/worked-solution "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)m_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2615/Source/Images/87ff4da53aaa0d24860e1ce4c87670e6.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x+2y$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2615/Source/Images/ded242afabd5cfa40778881b586cae44.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *smallest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *bottom left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2615/Source/Images/c48db414a7ab06b8e9aee0c509f1a013.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{ij} &= f(x_{i - 1}, y_{j - 1}) = x_{i - 1} + 2y_{j - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i - 1} + 2y_{j - 1})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151867 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd33f89c-3adc-5b57-b7b7-4877a9e54e11" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Decreasing Function"}] :question/id #uuid "3923e1d8-73e5-501c-96b5-d6b729260d3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6249" :question/problem "Let $R = [0,1] \\times [2,3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = 10-x^2-y^2$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$." :question/worked-solution "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)m_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2615/Source/Images/b4cb4b22e32666ef93b6b0cb8bdf196a.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) =10-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $x$ increases on $[0,1]$, and\n- $f(x,y) = 10-x^2-y^2$ decreases as $y$ increases on $[2,3]$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2615/Source/Images/c0d4216d9454a8c106a83aaca48b9e57.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2615/Source/Images/3c7f764f4959d3fad12cd0df70d46b14.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{ij} &= f(x_{i}, y_{j}) = 10 - x_{i}^{2} - y_{j}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1\\leq i\\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1\\leq j\\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(10 - x_{i}^{2} - y_{j}^{2})Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151969 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afd97f63-933b-5595-8b4d-01f14c3bff53" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Function That's Neither Strictly Increasing Nor Decreasing"}] :question/id #uuid "b3b3c2bc-029c-5128-8ea9-00591c6936d2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9496" :question/problem "Let $R = [2,3] \\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y) = x - y + 3$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nWrite down the lower Riemann sum $L(f, P)$." :question/worked-solution "The lower Riemann sum is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)m_{ij}Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $m_{ij}$ is the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion $R_{ij}$.\n\nWe consider an arbitrary subregion $R_{ij}$ of the partition. This subregion lies between $x_{i-1}$ and $x_i$ over the $x$-axis and $y_{i-1}$ and $y_i$ over the $y$-axis, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2615/Source/Images/4707fec393fa97f6a2a8bd379ea8574b.png)\n\nNotice that\n\n- $f(x,y) = x - y + 3$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n- $f(x,y) = x - y + 3$ decreases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2615/Source/Images/7d6f2f08ee2c75640b099bc91d36aef8.png)\n\nAs a result, the *minimum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *smallest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y$. This is the value at the *top left corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2615/Source/Images/9e0e5cae4e2067faf6025f08cbbd6685.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{ij} &= f(x_{i - 1}, y_{j}) = x_{i - 1} - y_{j} + 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor all\n\n$$\n1 \\leq i \\leq m\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n1 \\leq j \\leq n\n$$\n\nFinally, the lower Riemann sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{(}f, P) &= ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)(x_{i - 1} - y_{j} + 3)Δx_{i}Δy_{j}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152635 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "41a9d47a-2dd6-5177-afd1-e4adcd887f55" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Strictly Increasing Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87d4a79b-aec6-57a5-bbe8-4f2a16e97813" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i}^{2} + 2y_{j}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150721} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7d87d2-828e-539a-85eb-2fab92aa1239" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x_{i}^{2} + y_{j - 1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150722} {:answer/id #uuid "d06acb3c-9d69-53b0-a141-5a6ac69bb1da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x_{i - 1}^{2} + y_{j - 1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150723} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c6e74a-2d75-521b-b493-ada34132b9bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x_{i - 1}^{2} + 2y_{j - 1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150724} {:answer/id #uuid "152b1e58-73d2-5d67-9ffc-3f0525c08b20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x_{i}^{2} + y_{j}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d06acb3c-9d69-53b0-a141-5a6ac69bb1da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x_{i - 1}^{2} + y_{j - 1}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150723} :answer-field/id #uuid "eea8e9ff-1615-5e31-83ee-5d7bf940c1b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150720}] :question/id #uuid "5c8c4ecf-951c-581b-b162-445523199e67" :question/math-academy-id "q-75055" :question/problem "Let $R = [1, 2] \\cdot [2, 3]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 4x^{2} + y^{2}$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$L(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "afd97f63-933b-5595-8b4d-01f14c3bff53" :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Lower Riemann Sum for a Function That's Neither Strictly Increasing Nor Decreasing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b251e593-6487-58df-827c-e3780c8f492f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y_{j - 1} - x_{i - 1} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150733} {:answer/id #uuid "cadfebff-9bb6-5d6b-9f3c-13e378596cb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{j - 1} - x_{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150734} {:answer/id #uuid "00da48d4-7c52-5999-8015-370b9df43fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y_{j - 1} - x_{i} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150735} {:answer/id #uuid "2e2dec3a-9171-5936-9852-ca14fe15abfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y_{j} - x_{i - 1} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150736} {:answer/id #uuid "61f2fd7d-a587-59ba-96ff-5db7ce104ba7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y_{j} - x_{i} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00da48d4-7c52-5999-8015-370b9df43fb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5y_{j - 1} - x_{i} + 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150735} :answer-field/id #uuid "18ae3929-6ba1-59e9-8f48-59a800401205" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186150732}] :question/id #uuid "66b27849-faf7-5d65-90ba-66cd9d971131" :question/math-academy-id "q-75061" :question/problem "Let $R = [0, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x, y) = 5y - x + 3$.\nFurther, let $P$ be a partition of $R$, where\n\n$P_{1} = {x_{0}, x_{1}, …, x_{m}}, P_{2} = {y_{0}, y_{1}, …, y_{n}}, P = P_{1} \\cdot P_{2}$.\n\nGiven that the lower Riemann sum of $f$ with respect to $P$ is given by\n$L(f, P) = ∑_(i = 1)^(m)∑_(j = 1)^(n)AAAAAΔx_{i}Δy_{j}$\nwhere $Δx_{i} = x_{i} - x_{i - 1}$ and $Δy_{j} = y_{j} - y_{j - 1}$, what is the missing expression in the double summation above?"}]}
